Stage 13: this slice becomes a corpus consumer, and the corpus finds four
          defects byte identity structurally could not.

commit:    5a5eb71f (+ this slice)
corpus:    ffi/corpus/generated, 336 vectors, manifest corpus_version 1,
           validated against protobuf 7.36.2 (upb) and libprotoc 35.1
codec:     generated from generated/corpus.proto -- **rule 0** -- and never from
           corpus_superset.proto. See below for what that cost.
runtime:   the SAME Enc, Dec, W and OrderedMap the measured arms use. Only the
           types, the codec and the projector are corpus-shaped. A corpus that
           exercised a second wire reader would be testing the second reader.
arms:      run on all three of README 5.2's arms, and they agree exactly.

################ why this was a work unit and not an afternoon ################

CONTRACT.md's rule 0 is "generate your codec from `generated/corpus.proto`,
never from `generated/corpus_superset.proto`". The difference between those two
files is the corpus's entire unknown-field claim: a slice that generates from
the superset knows every field, executes no unknown-field skip, and passes class
`unknown` while testing nothing.

**This generator had no `.proto` front end at all.** It drives off
`ffi/schema/emit/shapes.py`, which reads a JSON description. So conformance
needed a second front end producing the same schema dict --
`gen/protoparse.py`, 140 lines, deliberately not a general protobuf parser and
raising on any construct it was not written for. After that every backend
already written emitted the reader view untouched: the facade types, the
structural comparer and the managed codec all took a namespace argument and
nothing else.

**The two front ends are cross-checked against each other at generation time.**
`corpus.proto` and `shapes.json` are both generated from one description, so a
field that differs between what this slice parses out of the .proto and what it
reads out of the JSON is a defect in the new parser. Nineteen messages and three
enums overlap and every attribute the backends read is compared; a disagreement
fails the generator. It found none, which is the reason to believe the parser.

One kind the schema does not have: `fixed32`, in `WireZoo`. It is now in the IR,
in the shared `Wire.cs` runtime and in all fourteen branches of the codec
emitter that had a `double` case. Adding it caught its own near-miss: two of
those fourteen sites are textually identical `oneof` branches, and a patch
asserting a unique anchor would have silently fixed one of them.

################ what the corpus found ################

Four defects, all in code that every gate this slice owns was passing.

**1. `X-tag-zero`: field number 0 was accepted.** The generated decoder's
`switch` sent tag 0 to the unknown-field skip, which happily skipped it. Zero is
the value a reader gets from an empty buffer it forgot to bounds-check, so
accepting it turns a truncation into a silently empty message. Now rejected as
malformed.

**2. `X-depth-101` and `X-depth-300`: no recursion limit.** A message nested 300
deep was 300 managed frames and a successful parse. Every protobuf
implementation caps at 100 and rejects past it, and ABI v1 open decision 7 says
no slice exercises this. `Dec` now carries a depth, the generated `Read*`
increments it, and past `W.MaxDepth` the parse returns `ErrDepth`.

  **Priced, because it is on the measured path.** One increment and one compare
  per message decoded. Against the unchanged incumbent arm, inside each process:
  managed-parse / gp-parse-seq on P1.2 is 0.725 before and 0.725 after; on P2.2
  it is 0.800 before and 0.763 after, which moved the wrong way for a cost and
  is noise. The encode control, which the change does not touch, moved 0.435 to
  0.428 and 0.292 to 0.293 over the same runs, so the run-to-run spread is one
  to two percent and the change is inside it. **No published figure moves.**

**3. `S-double-minus-zero`: minus zero was being dropped.** The omit-when-zero
rule was `!= 0.0`, and IEEE says `-0.0 == 0.0`, so a set field vanished. The
vector's own note says it: "the omit-when-zero rule must compare bits and not
value". Now `BitConverter.DoubleToInt64Bits(x) != 0L`, in the codec, the size
pass and the projector.

  `Google.Protobuf`'s generated C# writes `if (Field != 0D)` and therefore has
  the same hole. **upb writes the field and Google.Protobuf does not**, so this
  is a real cross-implementation divergence that the schema payload set could
  never surface: `ffi/schema` has exactly one `double` and it is packed.

**4. The group-skip hole**, already fixed in stage 12 on the same evidence.

And the reason none of this was reachable before: **byte identity is against a
manifest generated from the same description the decoder is generated from.**
No payload in `ffi/schema` can carry a field the decoder does not know, proto3
cannot express a group, the value rules never produce a minus zero, and nothing
in the payload set is nested past depth 4. Four of the five slices gated clean
on a core that had defect D7. The corpus is the only oracle in the branch that
is not generated from the thing it tests.

